      "text": "First, let's determine the value of \\( n \\) by using the information about the median. The median of the set \\(\\{n, n+5, n+6, n+9, n+15\\}\\) is given as 9. Since the set has an even number of elements (5 elements), the median is the average of the third and fourth elements. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+6) + (n+9)}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2n + 15}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the fraction, multiply both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\n2n + 15 = 18\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\( n \\) by isolating the variable:\n\n\\[\n2n = 18 - 15\n\\]\n\\[\n2n = 3\n\\]\n\\[\nn = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( n = \\frac{3}{2} \\), we can substitute this value back into the set to find the actual values of the elements:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\{ \\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{3}{2} + 5, \\frac{3}{2} + 6, \\frac{3}{2} + 9, \\frac{3}{2} + 15 \\right\\} = \\left\\{ \\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{13}{2}, \\frac{15}{2}, \\frac{21}{2}, \\frac{33}{2} \\right\\}\n\\]\n\nThe mean of this set is the sum of the elements divided by the number of elements. First, calculate the sum:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{13}{2} + \\frac{15}{2} + \\frac{21}{2} + \\frac{33}{2} = \\frac{3 + 13 + 15 + 21 + 33}{2} = \\frac{85}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe number of elements in the set is 5. Therefore, the mean is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{85}{2}}{5} = \\frac{85}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{85}{10} = \\frac{17}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the mean is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{17}{2}}\n\\]",
