      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The median of a set with an odd number of elements is the middle value when the elements are arranged in ascending order. For the set $\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}$, since there are 5 elements, the median is the third element. We are given that the median is 9. Therefore, the third element in the set, which is $n + 6$, must be equal to 9.\n\nLet's solve for $n$:\n\\begin{align*}\nn + 6 &= 9 \\\\\nn &= 9 - 6 \\\\\nn &= 3.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow that we have the value of $n$, we can find the mean of the set. The mean is the sum of the elements divided by the number of elements. The set is:\n\\[\n\\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the elements is:\n\\[\n3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 52.\n\\]\n\nSince there are 5 elements in the set, the mean is:\n\\[\n\\frac{52}{5} = 10.4.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the mean of the set is $\\boxed{10.4}$.",
